 and result storage\nwithout adding issue bubbles. No logged structural attempt has moved\nthis load-versus-execution selection before the MEM/WB boundary.\n\n\nHypothesis:\nReplace the two architectural-result fields at MEM/WB with one 32-bit\nwb_result register. Perform load-versus-execution result selection on\nits D input in mem_stage, using the current EX/MEM owner\'s mem_to_reg.\nwb_stage then exposes wb_result directly as w_data. This is the sole\nstructural experiment: move one result-selection mux across an\nexisting register boundary and consolidate its storage, with no new\nstage, changed instruction latency, or changed hazard policy.\n\nOn each existing !hold_wb capture edge, assign wb_result the value\nin.ctrl.mem_to_reg ? load_data : in.alu_result. load_data is the\nexisting byte/halfword/word extraction and sign-extension result;\nin.alu_result already selects the authoritative normal or divider\ncompletion bank. Use the older EX/MEM metadata, never a younger live\nopcode or divider response signal. Remove the obsolete registered\nalu_result and read_data fields from mem_wb_t rather than retaining\nduplicate functional result registers. Keep ctrl_t and its decoding\nintact; retaining mem_to_reg in the shared control bundle is harmless\nas long as no post-MEM/WB writeback mux still consumes it.\n\nKeep reset synchronous and clear wb_result to zero. A dmem hold must\nretain wb_result, destination, and forwarding eligibility along with\nthe existing MEM/WB payload while clearing retirement valid only.\nIn particular, never clear or qualify the retained result by valid,\nwb_w_en, or a bubble: a stalled EX consumer can still require that\npreviously retired writer\'s value. Capture bubbles on the same edges\nas before and preserve control-zero invalid EX/MEM entries. Regfile\nwrites remain gated by valid, reg_write, and the existing x0 rule;\nforwarding retains its current producer qualifications and EX/MEM\npriority. All consumers, including RVFI rd_wdata